    {
     "id": "/page/0/Text/16",
     "block_type": "Text",
     "html": "<p>In general, when <math>T = Q(1)^n</math>, there are finitely many possible decorations for each ray. Namely, for <math>\\rho = \\mathbb{R}_+(a_1x_1 + \\dots + a_nx_n)</math>, there are <math>2^{d-1}</math> many possible \u201csquare root subspaces\u201d <math>\\mathbb{C}(\\sqrt{a_1}\\theta_1 \\pm \\dots \\pm \\sqrt{a_n}\\theta_n)</math>, where <math>d</math> is the number of indices <math>i = 1, \\dots, n</math> for which <math>a_i \\neq 0</math>.</p>",
     "polygon": [
      [
       68.53999999999999,
       639.078
      ],
      [
       524.48,
       639.078
      ],
      [
       524.48,
       696.6242218017578
      ],
